; Two fixed pads separated by open water, with a single moving platform
; ferrying between them -- the only crossing in the stage (issue #19: the
; Ferry stage). It is a waiting game: whoever is on the dock when the barge
; arrives gets to cross, so both players want to be there.
;
; The gap is what makes this stage what it is, so the numbers that hold it
; open have to be stated, not implied. PadLeft's right edge sits at x=-300
; and PadRight's left edge sits at x=300: 600 px of open water between them.
; The longest reach in the weapon roster is the staff's max_reach = 240
; (resources/staff.tres), measured from the player's own centre, so a
; player standing at a pad's very edge and fully extending that weapon
; reaches out to only 240 px past the edge -- 360 px short of the far pad.
; 600 px is 2.5x that reach, not a near thing, and there is nothing solid
; out over the water to plant a swing on in the first place unless the
; barge happens to be under it.
;
; The barge (MovingPlatform, issue #18) is 160 px wide, authored at
; x=-250 and travelling 500 px right to x=250, both at the pads' shared
; y=380. At each end it runs 30 px in under the dock (its near edge reaches
; -330 against PadLeft's edge at -300, its far edge 330 against PadRight's
; 300), so the two tops are level and continuous for about half a second
; each visit, not just the one tick it used to be flush. The Barge node sits
; above the pads in this file so the docks draw over the part tucked under
; them. 4.545 s a leg keeps the old 110 px/s: slow enough that reaching the
; dock before it arrives is a real wait rather than a formality, and fast
; enough that a round is not spent mostly standing still.
;
; Eased in #59. The barge used to travel -220 to 220, flush at each end for
; an instant before turning straight round, so boarding or stepping off was
; a timing check with no slack. Measured with one push stroke from a pad's
; edge, the boarding window was about 1.0 s for the pickaxe, axe and dagger;
; the 30 px run-in adds 0.2-0.4 s to it (pickaxe 1.2 s, dagger 1.4 s), and
; the sword, which needs two or three short strokes, gets the same extra
; overlap to take them over. The 600 px of water and the cycle time barely
; change (8 s round trip to 9.1 s).

; Backdrop (issue #117): open water, a far coast.
